WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:12.450
Hi! Ice cubes, water in a cup, and the water vapour that goes into the air when clothes dry are all water! Today let's learn about the three states of water.

2
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.346
Water exists in three states: solid, liquid and gas.

3
00:00:17.496 --> 00:00:24.474
Solid water is ice. In ice, the water particles are packed neatly and only vibrate in place.

4
00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:32.082
In liquid water, the particles are close together, but they can slide past each other. That's why water can flow.

5
00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:41.178
Water as a gas is called water vapour. Its particles are far apart and move around quickly. Water vapour is invisible.

6
00:00:41.328 --> 00:00:46.242
All three states are the same substance: water.

7
00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:53.802
Ice has a definite shape and a definite volume. Whatever container you put it in, the ice cube keeps its shape.

8
00:00:53.952 --> 00:01:04.002
Water has a definite volume, but no definite shape. Pour it into a bottle and it takes the shape of the bottle; pour it into a bowl and it takes the shape of the bowl.

9
00:01:04.152 --> 00:01:12.402
Water vapour has no definite shape and no definite volume. It spreads out to fill the whole container.

10
00:01:12.552 --> 00:01:19.818
We can find water in different states all around us. Ice cubes and the frost in a freezer are solid water.

11
00:01:19.968 --> 00:01:23.994
Rain, rivers and the water we drink are liquid water.

12
00:01:24.144 --> 00:01:29.394
There is also water vapour in the air, but we cannot see it.

13
00:01:29.544 --> 00:01:35.082
When water boils, a white mist comes out above the spout. Is that water vapour?

14
00:01:35.232 --> 00:01:42.114
Actually, the small clear space right next to the spout is where the water vapour is. It's invisible.

15
00:01:42.264 --> 00:01:51.858
When the water vapour meets cooler air, it turns back into many tiny water droplets. The white mist we see is these droplets.

16
00:01:52.008 --> 00:01:58.722
Watch out! Many pupils say the white mist from a kettle is water vapour. That is wrong!

17
00:01:58.872 --> 00:02:05.178
Water vapour is invisible. The white mist is made of tiny droplets of liquid water.

18
00:02:05.328 --> 00:02:12.522
Remember: if you can see it, it's water droplets. Water vapour can't be seen.

19
00:02:12.672 --> 00:02:23.826
Your turn! You pour water from a cup into a bottle. What changes, and what stays the same? Pause and think.

20
00:02:23.976 --> 00:02:31.554
The shape changes to the shape of the bottle, but the volume stays the same. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:31.704 --> 00:02:41.202
Another one! Which state of water spreads out to fill its whole container? Pause and think.

22
00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:48.570
Water vapour, the gas. It has no definite volume. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:58.674
Last one! Does ice have a definite shape? Does it have a definite volume? Pause and think.

24
00:02:58.824 --> 00:03:07.074
Yes, both! Ice is a solid, so its shape and volume are both definite. Did you get it right?

25
00:03:07.224 --> 00:03:17.514
Remember: ice has a definite shape and volume. Water has a definite volume but no definite shape. Water vapour has neither, and it's invisible.

26
00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:22.770
Next time you see ice melting, think about its state!

